"""The development comparator table: each credibility configuration's stored attacker gains (D2+H3) and gross
efficiencies at the inner search budget, and the two configurations rechecked at full budget, from
experiments/v2/log/Cstar__*.json (development records, before the lock). Usage: comparator_table.py [LOGDIR] OUT.tex"""
import json
import sys
from pathlib import Path

ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[3]


def _name(c: dict) -> str:
    v = c["variant"]
    return "C-boinc" if v == "boinc" else f"C-mem({c['K']}, {c['M']})" if v == "mem" else f"C-{v}({c['K']})"


def rows(logdir) -> list:
    out = []
    for f in sorted(Path(logdir).glob("Cstar__*.json")):
        d = json.loads(f.read_text()); kind = f.stem.rsplit("__", 1)[1]
        out.append({"config": _name(d["spec"]["cred"]), "budget": "full" if kind == "recheck" else "inner",
                    "gain": {m: {fam: d["detail"][m][fam]["gain"] for fam in ("D2+H3", "X7-SC")} for m in ("headline", "dev0")},
                    "eff": {m: d["detail"][m]["D2+H3"]["eff"] for m in ("headline", "dev0")}})
        score, mk, fam = min((v, m, f) for m, fs in d["margins"].items() for f, v in fs.items())     # the objective: a minimum over markets and families
        out[-1].update(score=score, decides=f"{mk}, {fam}")
    return sorted(out, key=lambda r: (r["config"], r["budget"] != "inner"))


def _f(x: float) -> str:
    return f"\\ensuremath{{{x:.3f}}}" if x < 0 else f"{x:.3f}"


def table(rs: list) -> str:
    body = "".join(f"{r['config']} & {r['budget']} & {_f(r['gain']['headline']['D2+H3'])} & {_f(r['gain']['dev0']['D2+H3'])} & "
                   f"{_f(r['eff']['headline'])} & {_f(r['eff']['dev0'])} & {_f(r['score'])} & {r['decides']} \\\\\n" for r in rs)
    return ("\\begin{tabular}{@{}llrrrrrl@{}}\\toprule\n & & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{Gain, D2+H3} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{Gross efficiency, D2+H3} & & \\\\\n"
            "Configuration & Budget & headline & dev0 & headline & dev0 & Score & Deciding cell \\\\\\midrule\n" + body + "\\bottomrule\\end{tabular}\n")


if __name__ == "__main__":
    a = sys.argv[1:]; logdir, out = (a[0], a[1]) if len(a) == 2 else (ROOT / "experiments/v2/log", a[0])
    Path(out).write_text(table(rows(logdir)).replace("C*", "C$^*$"))

\paragraph{What they do not support.} The selected unconstrained welfare attacks lower the gross efficiency of all three core rules by a similar amount while earning less than the truthful reference against R3 and C$^*$; that is a loss relative to honest play, not necessarily a negative profit. Exploratory mixtures expose policies omitted by the prescribed W1 search that have substantially lower empirical efficiency at non-negative estimated gain; their population profitability remains unestablished, and the favourable W1 result for R3 and C$^*$ is not a lower bound on efficiency. Robustness to the searched families is not established: the doubling checks find missed improvements but cannot bound the remaining gap, and several decisions hold only for the prescribed search procedure. The fresh-market configurations differ from the registered ones in the last binary digit; re-evaluating the frozen policies and references with the registered configurations leaves every Q1 and Q4 decision unchanged, a check that does not repeat search or reference selection.
% END-EV2
\paragraph{Exact but costly checks.} The model's audit already reveals delivered quality exactly, at a cost charged to the operator's budget. An execution proof such as FlashProve checks a different object, that a committed computation ran, and does not remove delivery noise. It fits the model's audit cost only if the operator funds both proving and verification; provider-funded proving would need separate incentive accounting. Either way, which jobs to check and how to sanction remain the questions; if the cost of a proof fell close to that of the computation itself, the case for sampled audits would weaken. This discussion is outside the registered tests and changes none of their claims.

\paragraph{Limits.} The benchmark is a unilateral deviation against honest opponents; we claim neither equilibrium nor incentive compatibility. At the first-test parameters, Proposition~\ref{prop:terminal} establishes a profitable small same-offer shading deviation in a round with zero continuation loss under every studied rule. Exposure is bounded by the searched families and the optimiser. The fresh markets come from one stated neighbourhood, and the headline market was used in development. The honest opponents never replace their identities, so after the start a new identity marks a provider that has replaced; population churn of honest identities is untested. With such churn, newest-first targeting would spend audits on honest newcomers and could lose much of its targeting power, and the registered honest-replacement diagnostic covers a single replacement only. The comparator was selected on gross efficiency in effect, and the full-budget runner-up left a lower development gain.

\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}
% BEGIN-EV2
For the uniform-audit baselines B2 and B3 with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, against honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity, free replacement removes the ban's continuation cost. At the fixed parameters, the prescribed D2+H3 searches yield a lower measured gain for R3 than for B3 on every sampled market, but the registered gain-and-efficiency comparison passes on only three of six fresh markets, all three qualified by search flags. R3 and the development-selected verified-history comparator are pooled-equivalent within the pre-set margin in the six sampled markets under both Q1 families, which are close to one test; across markets the difference varies in sign, and this analysis does not establish the ordering for the neighbourhood. At the headline market, the selected unconstrained welfare attacks yield gross efficiency of about \site{c_w0} against all three core rules and earn less than the truthful reference against R3 and C$^*$. The registered W1 search found no profitable welfare attack (gross efficiency near \site{c_w1}); exploratory mixtures of a welfare attack and a profitable attack reach lower efficiency at non-negative estimated gain, so it supplies no efficiency bound for randomised policies. These conclusions concern frozen searches and references, with interval calibration admitted on development fixtures. The record of both tests, including the original failure, the amendments, the deviations, and the failed predictions, will be released with the paper.
% END-EV2

\paragraph{Availability.} The paper artifact will have its own release commit and archive hash. It identifies the frozen evaluation code by the signed tag \texttt{protocol-v2} and preserves the original tagged-code report. It contains the code, the tuned files, all records of both tests (including incomplete, failed, and cut cells), the market generator, the block headers, the released H4 bundle, and the deviation evidence, and, separately dated, the decision-stability re-evaluation, the exploratory post hoc analyses with the per-seed evaluation and training inputs of the lottery analysis, the prediction-scoring correction with its regression test, the inference audit, and the paper sources. One command (\texttt{docs/revision/deviation/reproduce\_deviation.py}) checks the artifact against the tag and reproduces the second test's report under the disclosed configuration substitution; the paper's second-test tables and figures are regenerated from the tagged report, the disclosed stability output, and the exploratory post hoc and audit outputs by the scripts in \texttt{docs/revision/paper-v2}, \texttt{docs/revision/posthoc}, and \texttt{docs/revision/audit}, and the first test's records and verdict are in the release as recorded in October~\const{2026}.
